/** \page cp-windows-and-dialogs Windows and Dialogs

The Window Manager manages the visibility of every window, dialog, and file dialog in Marmalade.

Marmalade provides the following types of Windows:

- \ref windows
- \ref modal-windows
- \ref dialogs
- \ref file-dialogs
- \ref native-ui-dialogs

\section windows Windows

Windows in Marmalade extend the `Marmalade::GUI::Window` abstract class. This requires the `Marmalade::GUI::Window::Draw()` method to be implemented.

The `WINDOW_BEGIN()` and `WINDOW_END()` macros should be used. These automatically handle visibility and plugin hooks.

```cpp
void Marmalade::GUI::MyWindow::Draw() {
    // Set default window size
    ImGui::SetNextWindowSize(ImVec2(800, 500), ImGuiCond_FirstUseEver);

    // Create the window. This example provides an icon.
    WINDOW_BEGIN(ICON_WITH_TEXT(ICON_CI_SETTINGS_GEAR, _("My Window")), ImGuiWindowFlags_None)

    // Draw content here

    WINDOW_END()
}
```

The window is stored as a field in `Marmalade::GUI::WindowManager`, and added to the `windows` vector.

```cpp
// windowmanager.h:
MyWindow myWindow{};

// windowmanager.cpp:
Marmalade::GUI::WindowManager::WindowManager() {
    // ...
    windows.push_back(&myWindow);
}
```

The window visibility can be controlled be either setting the `Marmalade::GUI::Window::visible` field, or by calling the `Marmalade::GUI::Window::ToggleWindow()` method.

```cpp
namespace Marmalade::GUI;

WindowManager::GetInstance().myWindow.visible = true;
WindowManager::GetInstance().myWindow.ToggleWindow();

// Or even directly through a menu item
ImGui::MenuItem(_("My Window"), nullptr, &WindowManager::GetInstance().myWindow.visible);
```

\section modal-windows Modal Windows

Modal windows are the same as standard windows, except they use the `WINDOW_BEGIN_MODAL()` and `WINDOW_END_MODAL()` macros.

```cpp
Marmalade::GUI::MyWindow::Draw() {
    WINDOW_BEGIN_MODAL(_("My Window"), ImGuiWindowFlags_None)

    // Draw content here

    WINDOW_END_MODAL()
}
```

\section dialogs Dialogs

Dialogs are similar to modal windows, but are registered differently.

A key difference in modal windows and dialogs is dialogs provide a callback on result, whereas modal windows are pretty much just windows.

A dialog extends the `Marmalade::GUI::CustomDialog` abstract class, which in turn extends `Marmalade::GUI::Window`. The
`Marmalade::GUI::Window::Draw()` method has to be implemented, as well as the `Marmalade::GUI::CustomDialog::GetName()` method.

```cpp
std::string Marmalade::GUI::MyDialog::GetName() {
    return _("My Dialog");
}

void Marmalade::GUI::MyDialog::Draw() {
    WINDOW_BEGIN_MODAL(GetName().c_str(), ImGuiWindowFlags_AlwaysAutoResize)

    // Draw content here

    if (ImGui::Button(_("Yes"))) {
        CallbackData data{"My data"};
        _dialog->Callback(true, &data);
        visible = false;
    }

    ImGui::SameLine();
    if (ImGui::Button(_("No"))) {
        CallbackData data{"My data"};
        _dialog->Callback(true, &data);
        visible = false;
    }

    WINDOW_END_MODAL()
}
```

The dialog callback contains two parameters: the result, and the custom data. The custom data is a `void *` so can be
casted to any data type.

When the dialog needs to be displayed, it is registered with the window manager, and shown.

```cpp
auto dlg = WindowManager::GetInstance().RegisterDialog(std::make_shared<MyDialog>(), [](bool result, void* data) {
    // Extract custom data
    auto* customData = static_cast<CallbackData*>(data);

    // Do stuff here
});

// Show the dialog
WindowManager::GetInstance().ShowDialog(dlg.Name);
```

\section file-dialogs File Dialogs

File dialogs are similar to dialogs, but the content is handled automatically by the ImGuiFileDialog library.

The `data` callback parameter is of type `Marmalade::GUI::WindowManager::FileDialogResult`.

```cpp
// If no path specified, defaults to project directory
IGFD::FileDialogConfig config = WindowManager::PrepareFileDialogConfig();
auto dialog = WindowManager::GetInstance().RegisterFileDialog("MyFileDialog", [](bool result, void* data) {
    auto* fileResult = static_cast<WindowManager::FileDialogResult*>(data);
    // Do stuff here
});

// Show the dialog
ImGuiFileDialog::Instance()->OpenDialog(dialog.Name, "My File Dialog", ".ext", config);
```

\section native-ui-dialogs NativeUI Dialogs

NativeUI dialogs call native operating system functions to display dialogs. This is useful if the GL system is broken, or
if you just want a consistent user interface.

```cpp
NativeUI::MsgBox::ShowMessage(NativeUI::Window::WrapGlfwWindow(GET_APP.getWindow()), u"My message", u"My title", NativeUI::MsgBox::Style::Style_INFO);

// Converting strings:
std::string someStr = "Some String";
std::u16string = NativeUI::Util::utf8ToUtf16Str(someStr);
```

*/
